# ============================ POS 3 ============================
LESSONS_M3 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Terminals as managed equipment", "10", "<p>This module covers everything between a bare computer and a working, licensed till: installation, the setup wizard, licensing, hardware, terminal settings, cached cashiers, and the device register. The module's law: <b>a terminal is commissioned once and managed for its whole life.</b></p><p><b>Treat terminals as managed equipment.</b> Every terminal in the business should have: a clear name, a branch it is locked to, a licence slot it occupies, a record in the device list, and a note of when and by whom it was set up. ZhiftPOS provides the tools for all of this — the setup wizard, the licence system, and the registered devices list — and this module shows how to use each one.</p><p><b>Why it matters.</b> Two problems occur wherever terminals are set up casually:</p><p>• <b>The badly commissioned terminal.</b> Set up in a hurry by whoever was free: named DESKTOP-7XK2P, locked to the wrong branch (or the right one by luck), hardware half-tested. It works until the first return, the first offline period, or the first question about which shop a sale belongs to.<br>• <b>The forgotten terminal.</b> Set up correctly and then never managed again: retired machines still holding licence slots, a lost laptop still able to post sales, a device list nobody reads. Module 8's fraud chapter notes what a forgotten, still-enrolled terminal is worth to the wrong person.</p><p>Both problems have the same fix: a fixed commissioning procedure at the start, and the device register for everything after.</p><p><b>Time expectations.</b> The first terminal at a site takes about forty minutes, including hardware. Each one after that takes about ten, because the decisions are already made and the procedure is already written. If your fifth terminal still takes forty minutes, the procedure is not written down.</p><p><b>The commissioning sequence.</b> The chapters follow the order you work in:</p><p>1. Install the application and connect the hardware (Chapter 2).<br>2. Run the setup wizard: server, connection, branch lock, updates (Chapter 2).<br>3. Activate against the branch licence (Chapter 3).<br>4. Configure and test the printer, cash drawer and customer display (Chapter 4).<br>5. Set the terminal's own settings and label templates (Chapter 5).<br>6. Cache the cashiers who will use it (Chapter 6).<br>7. Confirm its entry in the device register (Chapter 7).</p><p>Chapter 8 shows Okelewo commissioning five terminals across three branches, and Chapter 9 reviews. Keep a short file per terminal as you go — the wizard choices, the licence slot, the hardware test results. That file is the terminal's service record, and Chapter 7 explains where it pays off.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Installation and the setup wizard", "13", "<p><b>Installing.</b></p><p>1. Run the installer with rights to install software. The installer also installs the <b>background sync service</b> and sets it to start automatically with the terminal.<br>2. Connect the hardware now — receipt printer, cash drawer, customer display — and confirm the operating system can see each device <b>before</b> configuring anything in ZhiftPOS. The application can only use devices the operating system already recognises; configuring first and plugging in later is the cause of half of all hardware problems.<br>3. Name the machine at the operating-system level <b>to match the counter</b> — the same name the profile uses, for example LALUBU-TILL-1. This name appears in the device list and in every support conversation for years. Set it now, while it costs nothing.<br>4. Launch the application. It opens on an unconfigured sign-in screen.</p><p><b>The setup wizard.</b> Open Server Settings from the gear icon. The wizard has four steps.</p><p><b>Step 1 — Server.</b> Enter the back office's full address, including the protocol (https://...), then press <b>Validate &amp; Fetch</b>. The application contacts the server, confirms it is reachable, and downloads the connection details and branch list. If validation fails, check in this order: the address is typed correctly; the server is running; the terminal has a network route to it.</p><p><b>Step 2 — Connection.</b> The web port, real-time path and real-time port are displayed for confirmation. They are filled in from the server and <b>cannot be edited here</b>. If they look wrong, the server's own configuration is wrong — fix it there. The wizard does not allow a terminal to override the server's connection facts.</p><p><b>Step 3 — Branch.</b> Choose the terminal's branch from the fetched list and confirm. <b>This choice locks the device to that branch.</b> Notes:</p><p>• If the list is empty, press Reload. If it stays empty, either the branches have no warehouse assigned, or the server address points at the wrong system.<br>• Read the branch name carefully before confirming. A terminal locked to the wrong branch posts every sale to the wrong shop, and the error is discovered in the postings.<br>• After the lock, reopening Server Settings requires <b>administrator verification</b> on the terminal. This is deliberate: it prevents a cashier from re-pointing a till at a different branch or server. Treat any unlock as an administrator action worth noting.</p><p><b>Step 4 — Updates.</b> Choose whether the terminal downloads updates automatically. Recommended <b>on</b> for shops without technical staff on site. Changeable later in System Settings.</p><p><b>After the wizard.</b> The terminal is bound to its server and branch, and is ready for activation (Chapter 3). Record in the terminal's file: the OS name, the branch chosen, the updates decision, and the date.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Activation and licensing", "12", "<p><b>How licensing works.</b> Each <b>branch</b> is licensed for a number of devices. The first time a terminal signs in successfully, it registers itself against that branch's allowance and confirms its selling mode against the licence. There is no separate activation screen — the first sign-in is the activation.</p><p><b>Activation checklist.</b></p><p>1. Confirm a licence slot is free for the branch before commissioning day. The device list (Chapter 7) shows what is currently enrolled.<br>2. Perform the first sign-in yourself as the commissioning administrator, so the enrolment is recorded under a known account on a known date.<br>3. Confirm the till presents the expected consoles — this verifies the selling mode once, at the start (see the precedence rule below).<br>4. Note the activation in the terminal's file.</p><p><b>If activation is refused.</b> A terminal is refused when the branch's allowance is already fully used. The fix is housekeeping, not a support call: open the device list, find a terminal that is no longer in use, and <b>revoke</b> it. Revoking frees its slot immediately. Retired, replaced and lost terminals should already have been revoked — an allowance full of forgotten machines is the usual cause of a refusal.</p><p><b>If the licence lapses.</b> When the licence expires or cannot be validated, the terminal signs the user out <b>gracefully</b>. Two facts to know and to reassure the counter with:</p><p>• <b>Nothing is wiped.</b> The local data stays on the terminal.<br>• <b>No queued sale is lost.</b> The queue survives a lapse exactly as it survives a restart. The terminal simply stops accepting new work until the licence is restored.</p><p>A lapse is a commercial matter with a technical symptom: renew or revalidate the licence, and the terminal resumes where it stopped.</p><p><b>The precedence rule.</b> Memorise this sentence: <b>the selling mode on the licence takes precedence over the mode on the profile.</b> A branch licensed for Standard shows the Standard consoles even if its profile is set to Pharmacy. Two consequences:</p><p>• <b>When planning:</b> if a counter needs the two-stage Pharmacy flow or Booking mode, confirm the branch's licence carries that mode <b>before</b> building the profile.<br>• <b>When troubleshooting:</b> if a till shows different consoles from what the profile says, check the licence. Module 1's rule was: a missing menu item means check the profile. This chapter adds the second rule: wrong consoles overall means check the licence.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Know the branch's allowance before you start; activate under a known account; verify the mode once; revoke retired terminals to free slots; and remember that a lapse pauses the terminal without losing anything.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Receipt printer, cash drawer, customer display", "14", "<p>Hardware settings live in System Settings on the terminal, under the Printer and Display tabs. Configure each device, then test it the way described at the end of each section.</p><p><b>Receipt printer.</b> Settings, in order:</p><p>• <b>Enable printing</b> — once the printer is installed at the operating-system level.<br>• <b>Model family</b> — Epson or Star. This selects the command language. Most thermal printers emulate Epson; check the printer's documentation.<br>• <b>Installed printer</b> — pick from the devices the operating system reports. Press Refresh after plugging a printer in; a stale list is the most common cause of nothing printing after the cable itself.<br>• <b>Paper width</b> — 80mm or 58mm. Set wrongly, receipts wrap or cut off their columns. This is the single most common printer misconfiguration.<br>• <b>Copies</b> — one, or two where a signed copy is kept.<br>• <b>Print format</b> — Standard Receipt (compact thermal layout) or Invoice Style (fuller page, for business customers).<br>• <b>Font size</b> — balance paper use against readability.<br>• <b>Auto-print</b> — on to print every receipt automatically; off where customers usually decline one.<br>• <b>Header and footer</b> — shop name, address, footer lines, and optionally the goods return policy text (it has its own switch and a length limit).</p><p><b>Test the printer with a real sale, not a test print.</b> Procedure: sell one low-value item, print the receipt, then reverse the sale properly through Return &amp; Exchange. A test page only proves the cable and driver. A real receipt exercises the totals, tax lines, header and footer — the parts that matter in a dispute. Keep the first real receipt in the terminal's file.</p><p><b>Cash drawer.</b> Settings:</p><p>• <b>Enable</b> — only where a drawer is fitted. Enabled with no drawer produces harmless but confusing errors.<br>• <b>Connection</b> — <b>Printer</b> for the usual arrangement: the drawer is cabled to the receipt printer and opens on a pulse from it. (This is why a drawer that stops opening is often a printer-cable problem.) <b>Custom Command</b> for drawers that need their own instruction, entered as hexadecimal bytes from the drawer's documentation. <b>None</b> disables the trigger.<br>• <b>Test Drawer</b> — use it before go-live.</p><p>Operating fact: the drawer opens on completion of a <b>cash</b> sale. Module 4's shift routine depends on this, and module 8 notes that a drawer opening without a sale is something a supervisor should notice.</p><p><b>Customer (pole) display.</b> Settings:</p><p>• <b>Port</b> — press Refresh after plugging in, then select.<br>• <b>Baud rate</b> — match the display's documentation; 9600 is the common default.<br>• <b>Columns and rows</b> — typically 20 by 2.<br>• <b>Protocol</b> — the command set. Known symptom: text appears but never clears between items → select the protocol variant that does not send a clear command.<br>• <b>Line ending</b> — change only if lines overwrite or wrap oddly.<br>• <b>Port lock</b> — keep the port held open unless another application needs it.<br>• <b>Idle message</b> — the scrolling text shown between customers.</p><p>Troubleshooting rule for garbled or overlapping text: <b>change one setting at a time, starting with the protocol.</b> Changing several at once makes the working combination impossible to identify.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Label templates and terminal settings", "12", "<p><b>The profile/settings split.</b> Keep this distinction clear, because it routes every configuration question: the <b>POS Profile</b> (module 2) holds the counter's policy — what may be done. <b>System Settings</b> on the terminal holds this machine's own behaviour — how this terminal operates. The discount mode is a profile question; the paper width is a terminal question.</p><p><b>System Settings has five tabs:</b> Server &amp; App, Printer, Display, Sync &amp; Device, and Labels. Printer and Display were covered in Chapter 4; Sync &amp; Device is Chapter 6. This chapter covers the other two.</p><p><b>Server &amp; App tab.</b></p><p>• <b>Connection details</b> — the server, ports and branch from the wizard, shown read-only, with the validation status.<br>• <b>Inventory cache limit</b> — how many product rows the terminal stores locally. Rule: <b>set it at or above the catalogue size.</b> A limit below the catalogue means some items are simply not present in the local copy — and those items cannot be found while offline. When an item cannot be found offline, check sync first, then this limit. Raise it for large catalogues; lower it only on genuinely constrained hardware.<br>• <b>Full screen mode</b> — hides the title bar. Usual choice for a dedicated till.<br>• <b>Fast checkout</b> — completes sales instantly and posts them in the background. Leave it <b>off</b> at commissioning. Module 8 explains the trade-off and when to enable it; until then, the default is off.<br>• <b>Updates</b> — the wizard's step-4 choice, adjustable here. Keep the update posture consistent across the estate.</p><p><b>Labels tab.</b> Label templates control shelf-edge and product labels, printed from the Label Printing console (module 6). A template defines:</p><p>• <b>Layout</b> — name, price and barcode, or name and barcode only.<br>• <b>Label size</b> — width, height, and barcode height, in millimetres.<br>• <b>Type sizes</b> — point sizes for the name and the price.<br>• <b>Item code</b> — shown or hidden.<br>• <b>Sheet geometry</b> (for sheet printers) — paper size, the four margins, column and row gaps, and column count. A column count of zero means fit as many as the paper allows.</p><p>Working practice:</p><p>1. Start from a supplied preset rather than a blank template.<br>2. Adjust, then use <b>Save as new</b> — the preset stays intact and your variant gets its own name.<br>3. <b>Print one sheet on plain paper</b> and hold it against a real label sheet before committing label stock. One plain page costs nothing; a misaligned roll of labels is money in the bin.</p><p><b>Estate consistency.</b> Keep the terminal posture deliberate and uniform: the same cache rule, the same update stance, the same proven templates across the terminals of a branch. A uniform estate is why the tenth terminal takes ten minutes — and why any terminal can be diagnosed by comparing it with its neighbours.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Cached cashiers", "12", "<p>The <b>Sync &amp; Device</b> tab lists the cashiers this terminal remembers — the people who can sign in when the server is unreachable. Where the profile enables offline login (module 2), a cashier who has signed in <b>online on this terminal</b> is cached and can afterwards sign in offline.</p><p><b>The three states.</b> Each cached cashier shows one of:</p><p>• <b>Ready</b> — can sign in offline right now.<br>• <b>Re-login required</b> — the stored credentials were rejected. The cashier must sign in once while online to refresh them. Until they do, <b>their queued sales cannot post</b>. This makes a Re-login state urgent, not cosmetic: the person's completed sales are waiting on it.<br>• <b>Locked</b> — more than <b>seven days</b> have passed since their last successful online sign-in on this terminal. Offline access has expired; an online sign-in restores it.</p><p><b>The weekly rule.</b> Because of the seven-day expiry: every cashier should sign in online at least once a week on each terminal they use offline. Put this on the branch's weekly routine (module 8's calendar) rather than trusting memory — the cashier who only ever works through outages is exactly the one who gets locked out during one.</p><p><b>When a cashier leaves.</b> Use <b>Forget cashier</b> on this tab, and treat leaving as a three-record procedure, all on the same day:</p><p>1. Remove the person's row from every POS Profile that lists them (module 2's user table).<br>2. <b>Forget</b> their cached entry on every terminal they used. List the terminals from the device register rather than from memory.<br>3. Disable their back-office user account.</p><p>Why all three: a departed cashier whose cached entry survives on a back-room terminal can still sign in offline there, and sales made that way are attributed to them. Closing all three records the same day removes the possibility.</p><p><b>Manager override sign-in.</b> When an offline sign-in is refused because the terminal has no cached record of the cashier, a manager can sign the cashier in using the manager's own credentials. Two built-in properties:</p><p>• The sales made in that session are <b>tagged for audit</b>.<br>• The cashier is told about the tagging at the time.</p><p>Use the override as what it is — a way to keep a counter trading through a gap — and not as a routine substitute for proper caching. Because every override session is tagged, a monthly count of them is easy: the healthy number is near zero, and each one should have a known story. A branch that quietly runs on manager overrides because onboarding never cached anyone will show up immediately in that count.</p><p><b>Commissioning step.</b> When commissioning a terminal, have each cashier who will use it sign in online once, then confirm the list shows them all as Ready. That is the terminal's people, set deliberately.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The device register", "12", "<p>The <b>registered devices list</b> in the back office is the record of every terminal in the business. One row per terminal, carrying:</p><p>• <b>Identifier and label</b> — the label is the OS-level name from Chapter 2, which is why that name was worth setting properly.<br>• <b>Branch and profile</b> — what the terminal is bound to.<br>• <b>Enrolled by / enrolled on</b> — who performed the activation sign-in, and when.<br>• <b>Last seen</b> — when the terminal last communicated with the back office.<br>• <b>Status</b> — active or revoked.</p><p><b>Revoking.</b> Revoke a device when it is retired, replaced, lost, stolen or sold. Record <b>who</b> revoked it and <b>why</b> on the revocation. Effects:</p><p>• A revoked device <b>can no longer post sales</b>.<br>• Its history remains — the record is closed, not deleted.<br>• Its <b>licence slot is freed</b> immediately (Chapter 3's remedy for a full allowance).</p><p>The rule: no terminal leaves service without a revocation. A lost machine that was never revoked is still enrolled and still able to post into the business's records from wherever it is — the hardware version of a departed employee whose account was never disabled.</p><p><b>The last-seen review.</b> Once a month, scan the last-seen column for terminals that have not communicated recently. Three possible meanings, in rising order of concern:</p><p>1. <b>A closed counter</b> — seasonal or temporary. Fine; note it.<br>2. <b>A dead or retired terminal</b> — revoke it and update the register.<br>3. <b>A terminal that is trading but not syncing</b> — the serious one. Its queue is growing unposted and its prices are going stale. Investigate the same day: usually the sync service has stopped, or the network path has broken.</p><p>The monthly scan takes two minutes. Without it, case 3 is typically discovered weeks later, with a large queue to untangle and stale figures in between.</p><p><b>The terminal's file.</b> Everything this module produces per terminal belongs in one place — the file started in Chapter 1: wizard choices, licence slot, hardware test results (including the first real receipt), settings decisions, cached-cashier list, and later, any revocation. The register plus these files is the estate's memory: any terminal's history answerable without relying on whoever set it up.</p><p><b>Recurring tasks from this module</b> (collected into module 8's calendar):</p><p>• <b>Monthly</b> — last-seen review; licence allowance check.<br>• <b>On staff change</b> — the three-record procedure from Chapter 6.<br>• <b>On terminal change</b> — revoke with who-and-why; commission the replacement by the standard procedure.<br>• <b>Yearly</b> — walk the register: every row's branch, profile and label still correct; files complete.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: Okelewo commissions five terminals", "13", "<p>Okelewo commissions five terminals across three branches. The events below are the ones worth learning from.</p><p><b>The first terminal (flagship).</b> The old standalone till is replaced first, following the Chapter 2 procedure exactly: hardware connected and visible to the operating system before configuration, the machine named LALUBU-TILL-1 to match the counter, and the wizard run step by step. At step 3, the branch list shows two confusingly similar names — the flagship and a draft record for the second Abeokuta shop. Rather than guessing, the administrator pauses and <b>renames the draft branch in the back office</b> so the two cannot be mistaken, then completes the lock. Total time: forty-one minutes, as expected for a first terminal. A one-page commissioning checklist is written from the session; every later terminal follows it.</p><p><b>The licence refusal (Lagos).</b> The Lagos terminal's activation is <b>refused</b>: the branch's allowance is already fully used. The device list explains it in one look — a trial-era terminal, dead in a storeroom for a year, is still enrolled and holding the slot. It is revoked (reason recorded: retired hardware, never deprovisioned), the slot frees immediately, and the new terminal activates within the hour. Follow-up: a full census of the register against physical reality runs that week and finds one more ghost — a repaired machine that had been enrolled twice. Both corrected; the monthly last-seen review goes on the calendar with the operations lead's name on it.</p><p><b>The hardware tests.</b> The flagship printer configures as Epson, 80mm, and the real-sale test prints cleanly. At the second Abeokuta shop, the settings had been copy-pasted from the flagship — and the real-sale test immediately shows wrapped, broken columns: the physical printer there is a <b>58mm</b> machine. One setting change, one more test sale, fixed — caught on a test receipt instead of on a customer's. The Lagos cash drawer does not respond to the printer pulse; its documentation supplies a custom hexadecimal command, which is entered and confirmed with Test Drawer. The flagship's pole display shows garbled text on first connection; following the one-setting-at-a-time rule, the protocol is changed first — fixed on the first change. Label templates are adjusted from a preset, saved as new, and proven on plain paper before any label stock is used.</p><p><b>The people and the valve.</b> At each terminal, the branch's cashiers each sign in online once; the Sync &amp; Device list is confirmed all-Ready. At the flagship, the manager override is deliberately tested once: a cashier who had never used that terminal is refused offline, the supervisor signs her in on his credentials, and the audit tag is then verified visible in the back office — with the counter staff watching. The point of doing it publicly: everyone now knows the override works, and everyone knows it is recorded.</p><p><b>Result.</b> The fifth terminal takes eleven minutes and produces the same one-page file as the first. The MD's note: <i>I have bought machines for nine years; this is the first time I have owned an estate.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Review & the third law", "11", "<p>Check yourself against each section before the quiz.</p><p><b>Installation (Chapter 2).</b> The installer also installs the background sync service, set to start with the terminal. Hardware is connected and OS-visible <b>before</b> it is configured in ZhiftPOS. The machine is named at the OS level to match the counter.</p><p><b>The wizard (Chapter 2).</b> Step 1: server address with protocol, Validate &amp; Fetch; failures mean wrong address, server down, or no route — in that order. Step 2: connection details are read-only; if wrong, fix the server. Step 3: the branch choice <b>locks</b> the device; an empty list means branches without warehouses or the wrong server; reopening Server Settings afterwards requires administrator verification. Step 4: auto-updates, recommended on where no technical staff are on site.</p><p><b>Licensing (Chapter 3).</b> Each branch has a device allowance; the first sign-in registers the terminal against it. A refusal means the allowance is full — revoke an unused device to free a slot. A lapse signs out gracefully: nothing is wiped, no queued sale is lost. And the precedence rule: <b>the licence's selling mode overrides the profile's</b> — wrong consoles overall means check the licence.</p><p><b>Hardware (Chapter 4).</b> Printer: model family, refreshed device list, and above all the correct paper width (80mm vs 58mm). <b>Test with a real sale, not a test print</b> — the real receipt proves totals, tax and footer. Drawer: usually opens on a pulse from the printer (so check the printer cable when it stops); custom hex command otherwise; opens on completion of a cash sale. Display: baud, columns, protocol; text that never clears means pick the no-clear-command protocol variant; and always change <b>one setting at a time</b>.</p><p><b>Terminal settings and labels (Chapter 5).</b> Profile = policy; System Settings = this machine. Inventory cache limit at or above the catalogue size, or items go missing offline. Fast checkout stays off until module 8's judgment. Labels: start from a preset, Save as new, and prove one sheet on plain paper before using label stock.</p><p><b>Cached cashiers (Chapter 6).</b> Ready / Re-login required (their queued sales cannot post until they sign in online) / Locked (seven days). Weekly online sign-in rule. Leaving = three records, same day: profile rows, cached entries on every terminal (listed from the register), back-office account. Manager override sign-ins are tagged for audit and the cashier is told; monthly count near zero.</p><p><b>The register (Chapter 7).</b> One row per terminal: label, branch, profile, enrolled by/on, last seen, status. Revoke retired/lost/replaced devices with who-and-why — revocation blocks posting, keeps history, frees the slot. Monthly last-seen review; the dangerous case is the terminal trading without syncing. One file per terminal.</p><p><b>The third law: a terminal is commissioned once and managed for its whole life</b> — named, locked, licensed, tested with real sales, its people cached and removed on time, and its record kept from enrolment to revocation. Module 4 moves to the trading day itself.</p>"),
]

